inst/extdata/aligned.fasta, inst/extdata/aligned.nwk
  The "woodmouse" alignment distributed with the R package ape (Paradis E,
  Schliep K, 2019, Bioinformatics 35:526-528; GPL-2 | GPL-3): 15 cytochrome b
  sequences of Apodemus sylvaticus from Michaux JR, Magnanou E, Paradis E,
  Nieberding C, Libois R (2003), Molecular Ecology 12:685-697. aligned.nwk is
  a neighbour-joining tree of it.

inst/extdata/mtdna.fasta
  Sequences rebuilt by moose from PhyloTree Build 17 and the RSRS reference
  sequence (Behar DM et al., 2012, American Journal of Human Genetics
  90:675-684), both as distributed in the Haplogrep 3 tree
  "phylotree-rsrs-17" under the MIT License reproduced above. They are not
  sequences of individuals; see data-raw/alignments/build.R.

inst/extdata/pics/, inst/extdata/new-photos/
  42 photographs from the Fruits-360 dataset, 100x100 version
  (https://github.com/fruits-360/fruits-360-100x100, commit c3b8394),
  Copyright (c) 2017- Mihai Oltean, licensed under the Creative Commons
  Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International License
  (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/). See also Muresan H,
  Oltean M (2018), Fruit recognition from images using deep learning, Acta
  Universitatis Sapientiae, Informatica 10:26-42.
  The files are unmodified copies from the dataset's Training folders
  "Banana 1", "Blueberry 1", "Kiwi 1", "Lemon 1", "Pineapple 1" and
  "Strawberry 1"; those in pics/ keep their original file names, and those in
  new-photos/ are renamed unknown1.jpg to unknown6.jpg (one per fruit, in the
  order above). data-raw/photos/build.R lists the selection. These images
  remain under CC BY-SA 4.0, separately from the licence of moose's code.

The following third-party material is included under fair use (17 U.S.C.
§ 107). Each item is a small, attributed excerpt of a larger scientific
work, reproduced for non-commercial research and teaching: to test and
demonstrate software that converts identification keys into other formats.
Factual content (characters, taxa and test results) is not itself subject
to copyright; the excerpts are included for their structure, which moose
transforms rather than republishes as reading material. None of them
substitutes for the original work, and each is credited in the package
documentation with a pointer to the source.

inst/extdata/fishbase/key1-description.html,
inst/extdata/fishbase/key1-questions.html
  Saved FishBase pages (https://www.fishbase.se) for FishBase key 1, "Key to
  the families of sharks in the Western Central Pacific", entered by Rainer
  Froese (2001) from Compagno LJV (1998), General remarks, pp. 1196-1207 in
  Carpenter KE, Niem VH (eds.), FAO Identification Guide for Fishery
  Purposes. The Living Marine Resources of the Western Central Pacific. FAO,
  Rome. One key of the several thousand in FishBase; used offline by
  sharkKey() and the tests. The pages link to FishBase's morphology images
  but do not include them.

data/arachnida.rda (source: data-raw/arachnida/arachnida_key.txt)
  The 11-couplet "Key to the Orders of Arachnida" transcribed from
  Triplehorn CA, Johnson NF (2005), Borror and DeLong's Introduction to the
  Study of Insects, 7th ed., chapter 5. Thomson Brooks/Cole. A single key of
  22 leads from a textbook of more than 800 pages.

data/vibrio.rda (source: data-raw/vibrio/vibrio.tsv)
  The consensus identification matrix (74 taxa, 45 tests) from Noguerola I,
  Blanch AR (2008), Identification of Vibrio spp. with a set of dichotomous
  keys, Journal of Applied Microbiology 105:175-185,
  doi:10.1111/j.1365-2672.2008.03730.x. One table of results from the
  article; the article's text and keys are not included.

